ime by making lists of things  that are likely to happen  but in the back half of the talk i said  well we can discover analyze really fast  uh and these approaches enable us to get  into clinic  rapidly so antibodies off of these  platforms have already been into people  for chikungunya marburg source cov2  and others are bumping along in the  pipeline i do think in the future you're  going to see mrna  as a delivery mechanism certainly going  to be studied  and has promised to work as a fast  delivery  system and also you've heard lots about  vaccines and antivirals  and i think increasingly what you're  going to find is antibodies are going to  become the  the future of preventing and treating  infectious diseases one thing i didn't  mention is  um most of the trials that are being  done certainly with animals that we've  developed with  various partners have long half-life  mutations  and so some of the the actual molecules  are predicted  to have a half-life of 60 to 90 days but  the protective effect  could be projected as long as a year or  maybe even two we'll have to see from  the trials  but when you start getting in into  potential protection for a year  this is going to be competitive with the  type of protection you see from vaccines  so  increasingly antibodies are going to be  important in the space  and i've tried to mention collaborators  we worked with along the way  diamond barrick baruch dr lee's lab at  emory and  helen chu at seattle i have worked with  us  on samples and assays as  mario ostrowski who provided the samples  that  led to the lead drug candidates we've  worked with some great companies  berkeley lights the beacon i showed you  twist and the synthesis 10x genomics  work with us and these two companies id  biologics and astrazeneca are developing  uh clinical uh source cov2 we had  support from  american in europe mayor kgaa and  darvstad germany  with the future inside prize uh erica  sapphire and wilson andrew ward  alex mccraeff folks at integral  the mrna work we did for zika was done  with idri  uh in seattle and folks at moderna and  we've gotten funding from darpa but also  the nih  and being in nashville um you know it's  a music capital dolly parton  lives here uh and dolly parton made a  lead gift to vanderbilt to support  research early on the outbreak you know  to make a point of hope and we benefited  some from that  so thank you very much i'm happy to  answer questions  thank you dr crow for that informative  presentation and we will now start  the q and a portion of the webinar if  you have any questions you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen dr crowe we have a  quite a lot of questions coming in and  we'll try to get  as many as we can and any questions not  answered  will be answered via email first  question  what about anti-body from suspect  covid19  tell us a little bit  antibody from suspect  i i don't fully follow the questions say  it again  yeah what about um the audience members  says  what about the antibody from  um this from suspect covid19 or perhaps  maybe they're saying  do you suspect a certain antibody from  kubin19 or  not exactly sure how the question would  you like to answer that one via email  um sure i i  don't fully fully follow that question  all right  perfect um our next question is what  steps do you and your lab group take to  assure the safe  antibody treatment for pathogens and  viruses  so safety is is paramount we  are basically a discovery unit we do not  do manufacturing and we don't do trials  so  we license our antibodies out um  or provide them to various parties and  and they're  they're mostly focused on safety but  things that are done  toxicity testing is done in good  laboratory environments it's very  expensive  it's typically done in rabbits and  primates  tissue cross reactivity is examined so  antibodies are put on  all sorts of human tissues to make sure  that none of the  analytes are autoreactive or bind any  tissues um  and we also try to look at the predicted  half-life  in especially non-human primates because  you can get anti-drug  antibodies uh which would make antigen  antibiotic complexes  antibody antibiotic complexes which is  not good for you and also reduce half  life  so there are a lot of things that go on  during the early development stage  after discovery for safety purposes  thank you for that and we're  unfortunately getting closer to the end  of the time  i just want to ask one more question  before we go and give you time to have  some closing remarks  what is the difference between a wild  type virus  and a pseudo virus  that's a good question so the viruses  that are circulating in people  would be field strains once they're into  a lab and you've cultured them in cells  people  people call those isolates and they may  change in culture  but typically people also often will  call those  wild type viruses because they don't  change very much in culture  so that's a virus that you would use in  the biosafety level three if you take  another virus  that's not pathogenic for humans or less  pathogenic  such as vesicular stomatitis virus or  you could use  a debilitated hiv and you could take out  the  the naturally occurring surface protein  of the virus and swap in  source cov2 or some other virus that  becomes a pseudovirus  but usually they're debilitated by  deletion of an internal gene  so they can't make more virus they just  they they work as particles  so they can go in a cell and make a  signal but no virus comes out they're  much easier  safer to work with and and we work in a  biosafety level two lab  we don't work in biosafety level three  or four we do that with partners  so we oftentimes will use these  pseudoviruses that mimic the virus  but can be used at biosafety level too  and that's been very helpful to the  field  wow this is incredible stuff and i just  want to thank you 